Getting Started with DressCode
******************************

.[perex]
Install DressCode in a minute and try it on your own code without changing a single file. You will see where your PHP code differs from a coding standard such as [PER Coding Style 3.1 |https://www.php-fig.org/per/coding-style/] or Nette, exactly what a fix would rewrite, and what a newer PHP would bring to your code. DressCode formats your code and upgrades it to new versions of PHP and of your libraries, all in one run.


Installation
============

DressCode is a tool, not a library, so you can install it **outside the project** it checks. The simplest way is a global installation:

```shell
composer global require dresscode/dresscode
```

Add Composer's global bin directory to your [PATH variable |https://getcomposer.org/doc/03-cli.md#global], and you can run the `dresscode` command from anywhere.

For continuous integration (CI), where you want to pin the tool to an exact version, install it as a standalone project:

```shell
composer create-project dresscode/dresscode temp/dresscode
temp/dresscode/bin/dresscode check
```

The third way: you can add DressCode to your project as a development dependency (`composer require --dev dresscode/dresscode`) and run it from `vendor/bin/dresscode`. Choose this way once your project also has PHPStan: DressCode then asks it about the types in your code and can, for example, rewrite deprecated library API based on what the variable really is (see [Types from PHPStan |phpstan-types]). Keep in mind that DressCode requires PHP 8.4 to 8.6, so your project would have to run on one of these versions too, even if it could otherwise run on an older PHP.

This is the thing people mix up most often: **the PHP version the tool runs on and the PHP version your code is written for are two different numbers.** When DressCode is installed outside the project, it can run on PHP 8.5, for example, and check code written for PHP 8.1. It reads the target PHP version from the `composer.json` of your project and follows it, so it never writes syntax into your code that the target PHP version could not compile. This works from PHP 8.0; DressCode does not support older versions and tells you so.

DressCode has few dependencies: the parser and the tree it is built on are a standalone library, [PhpSyntax |phpsyntax:], with no dependencies at all. Besides that, it uses four small Nette packages, the phpDoc parser from PHPStan and the version comparator from Composer. No framework.


What Do You Want to Do?
=======================

DressCode does several jobs, and each of them has its own step-by-step guide. Pick the one you came for:

- **A coding standard for an existing project:** [Format Your Code Step by Step |php-code-style] goes from the first `dresscode init` to a standard that CI enforces for you.
- **A newer PHP:** [Upgrading to a Newer PHP |upgrading-php] shows how to rewrite code written for PHP 8.0 or newer to use what each later version brought, up to PHP 8.6.
- **New versions of your libraries:** [Upgrade Your Code Step by Step |upgrade] guides you through estimating, planning and running the upgrade; for [Nette |upgrading-nette], [Symfony |upgrading-symfony], [Laravel |upgrading-laravel] and [Dibi and Texy |upgrading-dibi-and-texy], DressCode has data on what changed between their versions.
- **Switching from another tool:** [Switch to DressCode Step by Step |migration] shows how to translate the configuration of PHP CS Fixer, PHP_CodeSniffer or Laravel Pint.

If you would rather see the tool at work first, stay here. In the rest of this page, you run three commands on your code, and none of them writes a file.


Check Your PHP Code Without Changing It
=======================================

DressCode has no style of its own, so without a configuration it does not start: if it quietly picked one of the standards, the first `fix` in a project written differently would rewrite almost every line. To try it, name a standard with the `--use` option and list the paths:

```shell
dresscode check src tests --use perCs
```

The output looks like this:

/--pre .[terminal]
DRESS|CODE 1.3.0
Config     none, using perCs
Target     PHP 8.2 from `composer.json`
Checking   214 files in /var/www/shop

src/Cart.php
  error   9:61  Expected a line break before the opening brace.         braces.position.function
  error  10:17  The array must be written `[…]` instead of `array(…)`.  literals.arraySyntax.array
  error  11:12  Expected no whitespace after the opening parenthesis.   spacing.padding.controlStructure
  error  11:20  Expected at least one space before the `==` operator.   spacing.binaryOperator
...

FOUND  36 violations, a fix leaves 1 in 12 of 214 files
\--

Each finding tells you where the violation is (line and column), what is wrong (the message describes how the code should look), and which configuration decision the code violates. The path on the right, for example `braces.position.function`, is a configuration key, and you can do several things with it: have it explained together with the values it takes (`dresscode explain braces.position.function`), [give it a different value |configuration], look it up among the [rules |decisions/braces#braces.position.function], or [suppress it in one place |suppressions-and-baseline].

The header at the top answers the two questions that cause half of all misunderstandings when left unanswered: what the code is checked against (the configuration file and the presets), and for which PHP version. The summary at the bottom also tells you how many violations an automatic `fix` would leave.

A message is a full sentence about one specific place. Where DressCode measures something, it says what it expected and what it found (`Expected the statement indented by 12 spaces, 10 spaces found.`). When fixing one violation would cause another one, the new violation is listed right below the one that causes it, marked `Then also`.

Such messages help most where more than the look of the code is at stake. Here are two small files checked against a few decisions from the `classes`, `mistakes` and `upgrading` sections and with [types from PHPStan |phpstan-types]:

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Settings.php
  risky  7:37  The parameter `$name` of `offsetGet()` must be named `$key`, as in `ArrayObject::offsetGet()`.  classes.overridingMethod.parameterName
               Risky because a call naming the argument `name:` stops working.

src/Users.php
  error  12:16  The `in_array()` call must pass `$strict = true`.                    mistakes.strictArgument
  risky  17:16  The `in_array()` call must pass `$strict = true`.                    mistakes.strictArgument
                Risky because a strict search no longer finds a value of another type.
  error  22:18  Method `Acme\Mail\Mailer::sendMessage()` is deprecated: use send().  upgrading.declarations.deprecatedMember
\--

The `Settings` class overrides the `offsetGet()` method of `ArrayObject` and names its parameter `$name`. PHP accepts that, but the call `$settings->offsetGet(key: 'theme')`, which works with every `ArrayObject`, fails with the error `Unknown named parameter $key`. Renaming the parameter, however, would break the calls that already use `name:`, so this is a risky fix, one that can change how the code behaves. That is why the finding for a risky fix says exactly what can break.

Both `in_array()` calls have the same message, but the fix treats them differently. On line 12 the types say that an `int` is searched for in a `list<int>`; there a strict search finds the same thing, so `fix` adds `true` by itself. On line 17 the value searched for is `mixed`: this fix waits until you [allow it |configuration#risky-fixes], and until then the finding only describes what it would change.

The library marked the `sendMessage()` method with the annotation `@deprecated use send()`. The finding quotes that advice, and `fix` writes `send()`.

The exit code is `0` when the code is clean and `1` when there are findings, so the same command can guard the code in [continuous integration |continuous-integration]. All four exit codes are in the [command line reference |cli#Exit Codes].


See the Fix Before It Happens
=============================

Add the `--diff` option, and the check also prints, below the findings of each file, the change `fix` would make:

```shell
dresscode check src tests --use perCs --diff
```

/--pre .[terminal]
--- src/Cart.php
+++ src/Cart.php
@@ -6,9 +6,10 @@

 class Cart
 {
-    public function add(Product $product, int $count): void {
-        $line = array($product, $count);
-        if ( $count== 0) {
+    public function add(Product $product, int $count): void
+    {
+        $line = [$product, $count];
+        if ($count == 0) {
             return;
         }
         $this->lines[] = $line;
\--

Nothing is written. Read the diff as you would read a pull request. If it changes things your team would agree with, the standard is close to your code. If it rewrites half of every file, your code follows a different style. You can try another standard by changing a single word: `--use psr12`, `--use nette`, `--use symfony` or `--use laravel`.


Preview an Upgrade to a Newer PHP
=================================

DressCode does not just format your code; it also rewrites it for a newer PHP. That is the job of the `modernizations` set, and you can try it alone, without a standard, so that the diff shows nothing but the rewrites:

```shell
dresscode check src --use modernizations --diff
```

```php .[before]
return $article === null ? null : $article->getAuthor();
```

```php .[after]
return $article?->getAuthor();
```

The set reads your target PHP version from `require.php` in `composer.json` and offers only what that version can run. Raise it and run the command again to see what the new version would bring to your code. This works from PHP 8.0 all the way to PHP 8.6, which has not even been released yet. [Upgrading to a Newer PHP |upgrading-php] describes what gets rewritten and how to do the upgrade in practice.


When You Are Ready to Write
===========================

The command that writes is `fix`, and it does not start without a configuration either. The `--use perCs` from your try was not saved anywhere, so a bare `dresscode fix` after it ends with exit code `3`:

/--pre .[terminal]
Error: No `dresscode.neon` or `dresscode.php` found in `/var/www/shop` or above it, so there is no dress code to check against. Run `dresscode init` to have one made to measure from the code, or name a standard with `--use`.
\--

You could repeat `--use perCs`, and DressCode would rewrite everything the diff showed. In a project with a style of its own, that is the big diff nobody reviews. A better first step is `dresscode init`: it measures how your code looks, picks the nearest standard, records where your code differs from it in `dresscode.neon` and keeps the first fix down to a few files. [Format Your Code Step by Step |php-code-style] goes through it, from the measurement to a standard that CI enforces for you.

One command writes without a configuration: `dresscode upgrade today`. After you update your libraries, it rewrites the code to their new API, and it can do so because it leaves the style alone (see [Upgrade Your Code Step by Step |upgrade]).


